  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Pick <strong>SimLocal</strong> if you want the cheaper plan, a real UK phone number bundled in, and a cancel-anytime refund policy. Pick <strong>Yesim</strong> if you're a repeat traveler who wants a dollar balance that rolls over between trips and a refund guarantee that covers plans you simply never got around to using. Both advertise an easy refund, but they mean different things by it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: Fixed Cheap Plan vs. Rolling Pay &amp; Fly Balance</h2>
  <p>
    SimLocal sells a straightforward fixed plan: pick a country, regional, or student eSIM, pay once, and it expires 14 days after purchase if unused. It sits in our "Cheap" price bracket. Yesim works more like a prepaid account: you top up a dollar balance (from $1.10/GB) instead of buying a fixed block, and whatever's left over rolls into your next trip instead of expiring. That flexibility puts Yesim in our "Expensive" bracket, since day-based plans run roughly $1.56 to $31 depending on destination, noticeably above what SimLocal or most other providers on this site charge for comparable coverage.
  </p>
  <p>
    Neither model is wrong, they're built for different travel habits. A one-off trip usually costs less on SimLocal. Someone who travels every couple of months and hates re-buying a fresh block each time gets more out of Yesim's balance that doesn't reset to zero.
  </p>

